---
import CourseLayout from '../../../../../layouts/CourseLayout.astro';

const page = { page: "lesson", paper: "paper-1", topic: "topic-2", unit: "2-2", lesson: "compilation-under-abu-bakr" };
const title = "Compilation under Abu Bakr &middot; 2.2 &middot; IGCSE Islamiyat";
const footerHtml = "Cambridge IGCSE Islamiyat 0493 &middot; Paper 1, Topic 2, Unit 2.2";
const mainHtml = "<section class=\"chapter\">\n      <div class=\"wrap\">\n        <div class=\"chapter-head\">\n          <h1>Compilation under Abu Bakr</h1>\n          <p class=\"ch-sub\">2.2 The Compilation of the Qur&rsquo;an under the Khalifas &middot; 12 AH &middot; the first mus-haf</p>\n        </div>\n\n        <p class=\"lede prose-col\">Less than eighteen months after the Prophet (pbuh) died, a single battle killed so many of the men who had the Qur&rsquo;an by heart that the first Caliph &mdash; a man who had just refused to do anything the Prophet himself hadn&rsquo;t ordered &mdash; changed his mind and authorised something entirely new: one single written copy of the whole Qur&rsquo;an, gathered under conditions strict enough that even a companion&rsquo;s honest memory wasn&rsquo;t enough on its own.</p>\n\n        <div class=\"box box-predict\">\n          <p class=\"box-label\">&#9633; Predict before you read</p>\n          <p>The previous lesson (Preservation of the Qur&rsquo;an) ended with hundreds of huffaz alive and pieces of the Qur&rsquo;an scattered across many scribes&rsquo; handwriting &mdash; but still no single official book, because revelation itself hadn&rsquo;t finished and the Prophet was still alive to confirm any question about wording. Before reading on: now that the Prophet has died and revelation has stopped completely, what do you think would need to be true before a compiler could be certain a verse belonged in the official text? Write down at least one safeguard you&rsquo;d want in place before trusting any single written copy.</p>\n        </div>\n\n        <h3 style=\"max-width:66ch; margin:40px auto 4px;\" class=\"prose-col\">The Ridda Wars and the false prophet of Yamama</h3>\n        <p class=\"prose-col\" style=\"margin-top:14px;\">Abu Bakr al-Siddiq became Caliph immediately after the Prophet&rsquo;s death in 11 AH (632 CE), and almost at once faced a crisis of a different kind: across Arabia, a number of tribes renounced Islam or refused to pay zakat to the new leadership, in a series of uprisings known as the Ridda Wars (&ldquo;Wars of Apostasy&rdquo;). Among the more serious threats was a man named Musaylimah, based in the region of Yamama in central Arabia, who claimed prophethood for himself in direct rivalry to Muhammad (pbuh). Abu Bakr sent an army under Khalid ibn al-Walid to confront him, and the resulting Battle of Yamama (12 AH / 633 CE) ended in a Muslim victory &mdash; but at a cost nobody had anticipated: a large number of the Qur&rsquo;an&rsquo;s huffaz, the companions who had memorised the complete text, were killed in the fighting. Traditional accounts put the number of reciters lost at around seventy, a substantial fraction of the several hundred alive at the time.</p>\n\n        <h3 style=\"max-width:66ch; margin:40px auto 4px;\" class=\"prose-col\">&rsquo;Umar&rsquo;s warning</h3>\n        <p class=\"prose-col\" style=\"margin-top:14px;\">It was &rsquo;Umar ibn al-Khattab who first grasped the danger this represented. His reasoning, preserved in Sahih al-Bukhari, was straightforward and urgent: the two safety nets described in the previous lesson &mdash; memorisation and scattered writing &mdash; had worked well while the Prophet was alive to confirm any uncertainty, but with him gone, if fighting continued to claim huffaz at this rate, parts of the Qur&rsquo;an could eventually be lost from memory with no living authority left to recover them. &rsquo;Umar went to Abu Bakr and pressed him to authorise a single official written compilation, gathered while enough huffaz were still alive to make it possible.</p>\n\n        <h3 style=\"max-width:66ch; margin:40px auto 4px;\" class=\"prose-col\">Abu Bakr&rsquo;s hesitation, and why it matters</h3>\n        <p class=\"prose-col\" style=\"margin-top:14px;\">Abu Bakr&rsquo;s first response was not agreement &mdash; it was refusal. His stated objection, as recorded in the hadith, was that the Prophet himself had never commissioned such a compilation during his own lifetime, and doing something the Prophet had not done felt to Abu Bakr like an unauthorised innovation in matters of religion. This hesitation is worth taking seriously rather than skipping past: it shows that the first compilation of the Qur&rsquo;an was not undertaken lightly or as an obvious administrative step, but only after real religious deliberation about whether it was even permissible. &rsquo;Umar kept returning to the argument until, in Abu Bakr&rsquo;s own words, &ldquo;Allah opened my chest for that which He had opened the chest of &rsquo;Umar&rdquo; &mdash; and he agreed.</p>\n\n        <h3 style=\"max-width:66ch; margin:40px auto 4px;\" class=\"prose-col\">Choosing Zayd ibn Thabit</h3>\n        <p class=\"prose-col\" style=\"margin-top:14px;\">Abu Bakr then sent for Zayd ibn Thabit, the young Ansari companion who &mdash; as the previous lesson covered &mdash; had served as one of the Prophet&rsquo;s principal Scribes of the Revelation and was reported present at the Prophet&rsquo;s final, doubled Ramadan review of the Qur&rsquo;an with Jibreel. Zayd combined exactly the qualifications the task needed: he was trusted, literate, had years of direct scribal experience taking dictation from the Prophet, and had himself memorised the complete Qur&rsquo;an. When Abu Bakr and &rsquo;Umar explained what they were asking of him, Zayd&rsquo;s own reaction echoed Abu Bakr&rsquo;s: he too hesitated at doing something the Prophet had not himself ordered, famously replying, &ldquo;By Allah, if they had ordered me to shift one of the mountains, it would not have been heavier for me than this ordering me to collect the Qur&rsquo;an.&rdquo; Only once Abu Bakr and &rsquo;Umar had persuaded him that the goal was identical to the Prophet&rsquo;s own safeguards &mdash; protecting the text from loss &mdash; did Zayd accept the task.</p>\n\n        <div class=\"pull\">\n          <span class=\"translation\">&ldquo;By Allah, if they had ordered me to shift one of the mountains, it would not have been heavier for me than this ordering me to collect the Qur&rsquo;an.&rdquo;</span>\n          <span class=\"cite\">Zayd ibn Thabit, on being asked to lead the compilation, Sahih al-Bukhari</span>\n        </div>\n\n        <h3 style=\"max-width:66ch; margin:40px auto 4px;\" class=\"prose-col\">Zayd&rsquo;s method: writing checked against witness, never one alone</h3>\n        <p class=\"prose-col\" style=\"margin-top:14px;\">What makes Zayd&rsquo;s compilation significant is not simply that he gathered up written fragments &mdash; it is how strictly he verified them before including anything. He did not treat a piece of writing as sufficient proof on its own, and he did not treat one person&rsquo;s memory as sufficient proof on its own either; each had to be checked against the other. In the hadith&rsquo;s own description, he searched out material &ldquo;from parchments, scapula bones, palm-leaf stalks, and from the memory of men,&rdquo; accepting a verse into the official compilation only where he could also find independent corroborating testimony &mdash; commonly described as requiring at least two reliable witnesses who had heard that exact wording directly from the Prophet. This was, in effect, the same two-track principle behind the Prophet-era preservation system described in the previous lesson &mdash; memorisation checking writing, writing checking memorisation &mdash; now being applied retrospectively, once, under enormous time pressure, to build a single trustworthy text.</p>\n\n        <h3 style=\"max-width:66ch; margin:40px auto 4px;\" class=\"prose-col\">The hardest case: the last two verses of Surah al-Tawbah</h3>\n        <p class=\"prose-col\" style=\"margin-top:14px;\">The clearest illustration of how demanding Zayd&rsquo;s standard was comes from the closing verses of Surah al-Tawbah (9:128&ndash;129). Zayd reports that he searched extensively and could not find these two verses written down anywhere except in the possession of a single companion, Khuzaymah ibn Thabit al-Ansari (referred to in some narrations as Abu Khuzaymah). By the letter of Zayd&rsquo;s usual standard, one witness alone should not have been enough &mdash; but the Prophet himself had previously and explicitly testified to this particular companion&rsquo;s exceptional reliability, effectively counting his single witness as equivalent to two. On that basis, and only on that basis, the verses were accepted and included. This single, carefully explained exception is itself evidence of how rigorously everything else was checked &mdash; it is remembered specifically because it was the one case where the normal rule had to be reasoned through rather than simply applied.</p>\n\n        <h3 style=\"max-width:66ch; margin:40px auto 4px;\" class=\"prose-col\">What the finished compilation was &mdash; and wasn&rsquo;t</h3>\n        <p class=\"prose-col\" style=\"margin-top:14px;\">The result of Zayd&rsquo;s work was a single complete manuscript, referred to in the sources as the suhuf &mdash; literally &ldquo;sheets&rdquo; or &ldquo;leaves&rdquo; &mdash; arranged surah by surah in the order the Prophet himself had specified during his lifetime, following Jibreel&rsquo;s instruction. It is worth being precise about what this was not: it was not a mass-produced book, not distributed to the provinces, and not intended as a public reference copy for ordinary Muslims to consult. It was one single, carefully verified manuscript, produced specifically to guarantee that the complete Qur&rsquo;an existed in one fixed written place that living memory could always be checked against, should memory ever again come under the kind of pressure it had just faced at Yamama.</p>\n\n        <h3 style=\"max-width:66ch; margin:40px auto 4px;\" class=\"prose-col\">Kept safe: the custody chain</h3>\n        <p class=\"prose-col\" style=\"margin-top:14px;\">Once finished, the suhuf remained in Abu Bakr&rsquo;s own personal keeping for the rest of his caliphate. On his death, it passed to &rsquo;Umar ibn al-Khattab as the second Caliph, who kept it in his own custody throughout his own rule. On &rsquo;Umar&rsquo;s death in 23 AH, the manuscript did not pass automatically to the next Caliph &mdash; instead &rsquo;Umar arranged for it to go to his daughter, Hafsa bint &rsquo;Umar, herself one of the Prophet&rsquo;s widows, who kept the suhuf in her personal, private custody. This detail is easy to treat as incidental, but it isn&rsquo;t: it was precisely because Hafsa still held this one authoritative manuscript, safe and intact, roughly a decade later, that the next stage of this story &mdash; &rsquo;Uthman&rsquo;s standardisation of the text for a rapidly growing empire &mdash; was possible at all.</p>\n\n        <div class=\"pull\">\n          <span class=\"translation\">&ldquo;Allah opened my chest for that which He had opened the chest of &rsquo;Umar.&rdquo;</span>\n          <span class=\"cite\">Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, on finally agreeing to authorise the compilation, Sahih al-Bukhari</span>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"timeline\">\n          <div class=\"tl-item\">\n            <span class=\"tl-date\">11 AH / 632 CE</span>\n            <h4>The Prophet (pbuh) dies</h4>\n            <p>Abu Bakr becomes the first Caliph; no single bound Qur&rsquo;an exists yet.</p>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"tl-item\">\n            <span class=\"tl-date\">11&ndash;12 AH</span>\n            <h4>The Ridda Wars begin</h4>\n            <p>Several Arabian tribes rebel against the new Muslim leadership; the false prophet Musaylimah rises in Yamama.</p>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"tl-item\">\n            <span class=\"tl-date\">12 AH / 633 CE</span>\n            <h4>The Battle of Yamama</h4>\n            <p>A Muslim army under Khalid ibn al-Walid defeats Musaylimah, but a large number of huffaz are killed in the fighting.</p>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"tl-item\">\n            <span class=\"tl-date\">Shortly after</span>\n            <h4>&rsquo;Umar warns Abu Bakr</h4>\n            <p>He argues that further losses could put parts of the Qur&rsquo;an beyond recovery unless a single written copy is made.</p>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"tl-item\">\n            <span class=\"tl-date\">Shortly after</span>\n            <h4>Abu Bakr agrees and commissions Zayd ibn Thabit</h4>\n            <p>Both men had first hesitated, seeing it as something the Prophet had not himself ordered.</p>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"tl-item\">\n            <span class=\"tl-date\">c. 12&ndash;13 AH</span>\n            <h4>Zayd compiles the suhuf</h4>\n            <p>Written fragments are accepted only when corroborated by independent witness testimony, including the special case of Surah al-Tawbah 9:128&ndash;129.</p>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"tl-item\">\n            <span class=\"tl-date\">13&ndash;23 AH</span>\n            <h4>The suhuf is kept by Abu Bakr, then &rsquo;Umar</h4>\n            <p>Passed on as each Caliph&rsquo;s personal responsibility.</p>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"tl-item\">\n            <span class=\"tl-date\">23 AH</span>\n            <h4>&rsquo;Umar dies</h4>\n            <p>The suhuf passes into the custody of his daughter, Hafsa bint &rsquo;Umar.</p>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n\n        <h3 style=\"max-width:66ch; margin:40px auto 4px;\" class=\"prose-col\">How a verse earned its place in Zayd&rsquo;s compilation</h3>\n        <div class=\"steps\">\n          <div class=\"step\">\n            <span class=\"n\">1</span>\n            <div><h4>Search</h4><p>Zayd sought out every known written fragment of that portion of the Qur&rsquo;an, wherever it existed &mdash; parchment, palm-leaf stalk, flat stone, animal shoulder-blade.</p></div>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"step\">\n            <span class=\"n\">2</span>\n            <div><h4>Corroborate</h4><p>He required independent testimony from those who had heard that exact wording recited directly by the Prophet, not the written fragment alone.</p></div>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"step\">\n            <span class=\"n\">3</span>\n            <div><h4>Cross-check the order</h4><p>He confirmed the verse&rsquo;s placement within its surah against what the Prophet himself had specified, following Jibreel&rsquo;s instruction, rather than deciding order himself.</p></div>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"step\">\n            <span class=\"n\">4</span>\n            <div><h4>Resolve exceptions carefully</h4><p>Where corroboration was thinner than usual, as with Surah al-Tawbah 9:128&ndash;129, he relied on the Prophet&rsquo;s own prior testimony to a witness&rsquo;s exceptional reliability, rather than lowering the standard generally.</p></div>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"step\">\n            <span class=\"n\">5</span>\n            <div><h4>Add to the single manuscript</h4><p>Only once satisfied did the verse take its confirmed place in the growing suhuf.</p></div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"box box-source\">\n          <p class=\"box-label\">&#9633; Where this comes from</p>\n          <p>This lesson&rsquo;s core narrative &mdash; the Ridda Wars and Battle of Yamama, &rsquo;Umar&rsquo;s warning and Abu Bakr&rsquo;s initial refusal, Zayd ibn Thabit&rsquo;s own hesitation and his &ldquo;moving a mountain&rdquo; quotation, his corroboration method, the Surah al-Tawbah 9:128&ndash;129 case involving Khuzaymah ibn Thabit al-Ansari, and the suhuf&rsquo;s custody chain from Abu Bakr to &rsquo;Umar to Hafsa &mdash; is drawn from Sahih al-Bukhari, Kitab Fada&rsquo;il al-Qur&rsquo;an (&ldquo;The Book of the Virtues of the Qur&rsquo;an&rdquo;), the hadith narrated by Zayd ibn Thabit, cross-checked via sunnah.com. The wider historical framing of this as the first of two distinct compilation stages draws on the Yaqeen Institute&rsquo;s research paper <em>The &rsquo;Uthmanic Codex</em>, already used elsewhere in this project&rsquo;s Topic 2.1&ndash;2.3 textbook chapter on this subject.</p>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"box box-source\">\n          <p class=\"box-label\">&#9633; A gap in this project&rsquo;s Yasir Qadhi extraction, and how it&rsquo;s handled</p>\n          <p>Abu Ammaar Yasir Qadhi&rsquo;s <em>An Introduction to the Sciences of the Qur&rsquo;aan</em> (Al-Hidaayah, 1999) covers Abu Bakr&rsquo;s compilation in Chapter 8, &ldquo;Compilation of the Qur&rsquo;aan,&rdquo; Section II &mdash; but, as flagged in the previous two lessons, this project&rsquo;s extraction of the book only reaches to roughly page 80, short of that chapter. This lesson is built instead from the primary hadith material in Sahih al-Bukhari and the Yaqeen Institute&rsquo;s dedicated compilation-history paper, both standard, cross-checked sources for this exact narrative. If a fuller extraction of Qadhi&rsquo;s book becomes available, this lesson is worth checking against his own wording for style consistency, though the underlying facts are not contested between sources.</p>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"box box-source\">\n          <p class=\"box-label\">&#9633; Common mix-up to avoid &mdash; this lesson stops at Hafsa&rsquo;s custody, not &rsquo;Uthman</p>\n          <p>This lesson covers only Abu Bakr&rsquo;s compilation &mdash; the crisis of Yamama, Zayd&rsquo;s method, and the suhuf&rsquo;s safekeeping through &rsquo;Umar and finally Hafsa. It does not cover &rsquo;Uthman&rsquo;s later standardisation, Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman&rsquo;s warning, the four-man committee, the Quraysh-dialect rule, or the destruction of rival personal copies &mdash; all of which belong to the next lesson, &ldquo;Compilation under &rsquo;Uthman.&rdquo; If an exam question specifically names &rsquo;Uthman, or asks about multiple standard copies being distributed to different regions, it needs that next lesson&rsquo;s content, not this one. A question asking generally about &ldquo;how the Qur&rsquo;an was compiled and preserved&rdquo; needs both lessons combined &mdash; see that lesson&rsquo;s Exam Lab entries for the complete, exam-ready answers to those broader questions.</p>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"box box-elaborate\">\n          <p class=\"box-label\">&#9633; Elaborate</p>\n          <p>Look back at your Predict Box answer. You were asked what safeguard you&rsquo;d want in place before trusting a single written copy, now that the Prophet could no longer be asked to confirm anything. Compare your guess with Zayd&rsquo;s actual method &mdash; requiring independent witness corroboration for every piece of writing. Now explain in your own words why this made Abu Bakr&rsquo;s compilation fundamentally different from simply asking one trusted scribe to write out the Qur&rsquo;an from his own memory alone.</p>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"box box-elaborate\">\n          <p class=\"box-label\">&#9633; Elaborate</p>\n          <p>Both Abu Bakr and Zayd ibn Thabit hesitated before agreeing to compile the Qur&rsquo;an, on the same grounds &mdash; that the Prophet himself had never ordered it. Write a short paragraph explaining why their shared hesitation should actually increase, rather than decrease, a student&rsquo;s confidence that the resulting suhuf was produced carefully and conscientiously, rather than casually.</p>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"examlab\">\n          <div class=\"examlab-head\">\n            <div>\n              <div class=\"tag\">PAST PAPER &middot; 0493/12 &middot; PAPER 1, Q2 &middot; MAY/JUNE 2021 (J21 S12)</div>\n              <h4>(a) Roles of Abu Bakr, &rsquo;Umar and &rsquo;Uthman in compiling and preserving the Qur&rsquo;an. <span class=\"marks\">10 marks</span></h4>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"examlab-body\">\n            <div class=\"ao-block\">\n              <p class=\"ao-label\">AO1 &middot; model answer points</p>\n              <ol>\n                <li>Abu Bakr: after the Battle of Yamama killed a large number of huffaz, he was persuaded by &rsquo;Umar to commission a single written compilation, despite his own initial hesitation that the Prophet had not himself ordered it.</li>\n                <li>Abu Bakr appointed Zayd ibn Thabit to lead the compilation.</li>\n                <li>&rsquo;Umar: first to raise the alarm after Yamama and to persuade Abu Bakr; later kept the completed suhuf in his own custody as the second Caliph.</li>\n                <li>On &rsquo;Umar&rsquo;s death, the suhuf passed to his daughter Hafsa bint &rsquo;Umar for safekeeping.</li>\n                <li>(For &rsquo;Uthman&rsquo;s role and the remaining AO1 points needed for full marks on this question, see the Exam Lab entry in &ldquo;Compilation under &rsquo;Uthman.&rdquo;)</li>\n              </ol>\n            </div>\n            <p style=\"color:var(--ink-soft);\">Part (b) &mdash; &ldquo;Significance of the Qur&rsquo;an being written as a book&rdquo; (4 marks) &mdash; a written book gave the Qur&rsquo;an a fixed, checkable physical form independent of any one person&rsquo;s memory, guarding against the kind of loss shown by the Yamama huffaz deaths, and gave every generation since a single reliable reference point that memory alone could never have guaranteed on its own.</p>\n            <div class=\"examiner-note\"><strong>Examiner note:</strong> This question needs both this lesson and &ldquo;Compilation under &rsquo;Uthman&rdquo; combined for full AO1 marks &mdash; an answer covering Abu Bakr and &rsquo;Umar in detail but omitting &rsquo;Uthman entirely will lose a significant share of the marks. Use this lesson&rsquo;s points as the opening third of your answer, not the whole answer.</div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"examlab\">\n          <div class=\"examlab-head\">\n            <div>\n              <div class=\"tag\">PAST PAPER &middot; 0493/12 &middot; PAPER 1, Q2 &middot; OCT/NOV 2022 (N22 W12)</div>\n              <h4>(a) Ways the Qur&rsquo;an was compiled and preserved after the Prophet&rsquo;s death. <span class=\"marks\">10 marks</span></h4>\n            </div>\n          </div>\n          <div class=\"examlab-body\">\n            <div class=\"ao-block\">\n              <p class=\"ao-label\">AO1 &middot; model answer points</p>\n              <ol>\n                <li>After the Battle of Yamama killed a large number of huffaz, &rsquo;Umar persuaded Abu Bakr to commission a written compilation of the whole Qur&rsquo;an.</li>\n                <li>Zayd ibn Thabit led the compilation, accepting written material only when corroborated by independent witness testimony &mdash; for example the closing verses of Surah al-Tawbah, verified through Khuzaymah ibn Thabit.</li>\n                <li>The resulting suhuf was kept safe, passing from Abu Bakr to &rsquo;Umar and then to Hafsa bint &rsquo;Umar.</li>\n                <li>(For the second stage &mdash; &rsquo;Uthman&rsquo;s standardisation &mdash; see the Exam Lab entry in &ldquo;Compilation under &rsquo;Uthman,&rdquo; which this question also requires for full marks.)</li>\n              </ol>\n            </div>\n            <p style=\"color:var(--ink-soft);\">Part (b) &mdash; &ldquo;How useful is it that Muslims today can access the Qur&rsquo;an in many formats?&rdquo; (4 marks) &mdash; modern formats (print, audio recitation, apps, searchable digital text) make the Qur&rsquo;an instantly accessible and support memorisation through repeated listening &mdash; but this only works because a single agreed text was fixed centuries ago; the full reasoning for this point depends on &rsquo;Uthman&rsquo;s standardisation, covered in the next lesson.</p>\n            <div class=\"examiner-note\"><strong>Examiner note:</strong> Treat this lesson&rsquo;s points as roughly the first half of a full-marks AO1 answer &mdash; the examiner is looking for both the Abu Bakr and the &rsquo;Uthman stage of &ldquo;after the Prophet&rsquo;s death.&rdquo;</div>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n\n        <div style=\"max-width:66ch; margin:40px auto 0;\" class=\"prose-col\">\n          <h3 style=\"display:flex; align-items:baseline; gap:12px; flex-wrap:wrap;\">Ready to practise?</h3>\n          <p style=\"margin-top:10px; color:var(--ink-soft);\">A short Practice Quiz first, then a graded Quiz once you&rsquo;re confident.</p>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"lesson-pager\">\n          <a class=\"pager-link\" href=\"../preservation-before-compilation/quiz.html\"><span class=\"pg-label\">&larr; Back</span><div class=\"pg-title\">Preservation Before Compilation Quiz</div></a>\n          <a class=\"pager-link next\" href=\"practice.html\"><span class=\"pg-label\">Next &rarr;</span><div class=\"pg-title\">Practice Quiz</div></a>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </section>";
